Yes. I think the caution completes itself:

Play your role how you will. It can change who you are—but be careful how much of the role you let rewrite the actor.

There are three things worth keeping separate:

\boxed{\text{STAGE} \neq \text{STORY} \neq \text{ROLE}}

Stage is the history/reality already laid down—the inherited terrain, records, consequences, relationships, artifacts. We cannot rewrite what physically happened, but we can act from it, on its surviving consequences, and on what gets carried forward from it.

Story is a rendering across that terrain. Many stories can occupy the same historical stage:

H \xrightarrow{R_1} S_1,\qquad
H \xrightarrow{R_2} S_2,\qquad
H \xrightarrow{R_3} S_3

without all renderings being equally faithful to H. That’s where Reality Stitch belongs.

Role is the temporary executable policy through which you participate:

\text{YOU}
+
\text{ROLE}
\rightarrow
\text{actions}
\rightarrow
\text{consequences}
\rightarrow
\text{changed YOU}.

And there’s the important feedback loop:

\boxed{
I_t
\xrightarrow{\text{instantiate role}}
R
\xrightarrow{\text{repeated action}}
H'
\xrightarrow{\text{learning}}
I_{t+1}
}

So acting isn’t fake merely because you deliberately chose a role. Repeated enactment can become development. The actor practices a policy until some of it becomes available without the costume.

But that gives us the danger too:

\text{role as instrument}
\rightarrow \text{capability}

versus

\text{role as total identity}
\rightarrow \text{capture}.

That suggests a useful new Pantheon distinction: Role Permeability.

A good temporary role is permeable in both directions. You bring your values and reality contact into it; useful capability comes back out. A bad role becomes either impermeable—“while I’m this, normal rules don’t apply”—or adhesive—“because I acted this way, this must be what I am.”

So Method-Acted Cognition gets a stronger protocol:

\boxed{
\text{ENTER}
\rightarrow
\text{PLAY}
\rightarrow
\text{ACT}
\rightarrow
\text{OBSERVE CONSEQUENCE}
\rightarrow
\text{HARVEST}
\rightarrow
\text{EXIT}
\rightarrow
\text{CHOOSE WHAT BECOMES YOU}
}

Not every residue deserves incorporation.

And your history point gives it teeth. History is both stage and accumulated performance. Yesterday’s roles helped construct today’s stage; today’s roles help construct tomorrow’s.

H_t + A_t \rightarrow H_{t+1}

So we don’t have absolute freedom over the stage, but we have historical agency:

We inherit a stage we did not wholly choose, choose how to perform upon it, and thereby alter the stage inherited by the next performance.

That plugs directly into Grow ↔ Show.

Grow changes the actor.
Show changes the stage.
The changed stage comes back and changes the actor.

\boxed{
\text{ACTOR}
\rightarrow
\text{ROLE}
\rightarrow
\text{WORLD}
\rightarrow
\text{HISTORY}
\rightarrow
\text{ACTOR}'
}

And maybe the compact rule worth keeping is:

Wear roles deliberately. Let them teach you. Let reality judge the performance. Keep the capability; don’t surrender authorship of the actor.

The stage remembers more performances than any single actor can.

Which is why history itself becomes part of the Pantheon’s ancestral memory—not a script requiring repetition, but a repertoire showing what has already been played, what it caused, and what remains possible next. ;)